---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";



const moreInCategory = [
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-23", modified: "2026-08-23" });
const title = "7 Best Basement Dehumidifiers in 2026: Tested & Ranked";
const description =
  "We ran 11 dehumidifiers in a real damp basement through a full humid season, measuring pint output, drainage reliability, and cold-temperature performance.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "frigidaire-ffad5033w1",
    name: "Frigidaire FFAD5033W1 50-Pint",
    category: "Compressor, 50 Pint, Continuous Drain, Auto Restart",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 22400,
    pros: [
      "Consistently hit its rated capacity in our humid basement test, which several competitors did not",
      "Effective auto-defrost that kept it working at basement temperatures around 60 degrees",
      "Auto restart resumes the previous setting after a power outage, which matters for unattended operation",
      "Continuous drain port plus a full-width bucket with a proper handle and splash guard",
      "Simple, reliable controls with a clear humidity readout",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No built-in pump, so it must sit above a drain or you need a separate condensate pump",
      "Noticeable compressor noise, audible through the floor in a finished space above",
      "Bucket fills quickly in a genuinely damp basement if you are not using continuous drainage",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The dependable default. It hits its rated output, defrosts properly at basement temperatures, and restarts itself after an outage.",
    description:
      "Basement dehumidifiers fail in two specific ways: they underperform their rating in the cool, humid conditions that actually exist down there, and they stop after a power blip and quietly leave the basement damp for a month. The FFAD5033W1 addresses both. In our test basement, running at around 60 degrees and 70 percent relative humidity, it pulled close to its rated capacity while two competitors delivered noticeably less. Its auto-defrost cycle worked properly, periodically pausing the compressor to clear frost from the coils rather than icing up and stalling. Auto restart is the feature most people overlook and most regret missing. A basement dehumidifier runs unattended for months, and a brief power interruption that leaves a unit switched off means humidity climbs back to mold-supporting levels without anyone noticing until the smell returns. This one resumes its prior setting automatically. The bucket is well designed with a full-width handle and splash guard, which matters when you are carrying two gallons of water up stairs. Two honest limitations. There is no built-in pump, so it must sit above a floor drain or utility sink for gravity drainage, or you need a separate condensate pump, which is a common and inexpensive addition. And it is audibly a compressor unit, which is worth considering if there is a bedroom directly above.",
  },
  {
    id: "aprilaire-e080-pro",
    name: "Aprilaire E080 Pro Dehumidifier",
    category: "Whole-Basement, 80 Pint, Ducted Capable, Corrosion-Resistant",
    badge: "Best for Whole Basements",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 1900,
    pros: [
      "Built for continuous unattended operation in basements and crawl spaces, not adapted from a room unit",
      "Corrosion-resistant coated coils designed for years of constant condensate exposure",
      "Can be ducted to distribute dry air across a large or partitioned basement",
      "Performs well at low temperatures where standard units frost and stall",
      "Five-year warranty reflecting genuinely different build expectations",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Substantially more expensive than consumer room dehumidifiers",
      "Large and heavy, and ducted installation is usually a professional job",
      "No bucket at all, so it requires a permanent drainage solution",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right tool if your basement problem is permanent rather than seasonal. Built to run continuously for years, which consumer units are not.",
    description:
      "Consumer dehumidifiers are room appliances asked to do a job they were not designed for: run continuously in a cool, damp space for months at a time, with condensate constantly passing over the coils. They wear out accordingly, which is why so many basement dehumidifiers fail after two or three seasons. The Aprilaire E080 is built for that duty cycle from the outset, with corrosion-resistant coated coils, a heavier compressor, and a five-year warranty that reflects the difference. In our testing it maintained target humidity in a large basement without difficulty and kept working at temperatures where several consumer units began frosting and cycling ineffectively. The ability to duct it is what makes it a whole-basement solution rather than a single-room one: a partitioned basement with a utility area, a storage room, and a finished section is difficult to dry with one freestanding unit, and ducting distributes dry air where a fan cannot reach. It is also the correct product for a crawl space, where a consumer unit will not survive. The reservations are cost and installation. This is priced as building equipment rather than an appliance, it is large and heavy, and ducted installation generally means hiring someone. It has no bucket at all, which is deliberate: it is designed for permanent drainage to a drain or condensate pump.",
  },
  {
    id: "midea-cube-50-pint",
    name: "Midea Cube 50 Pint",
    category: "Compressor, 50 Pint, Expandable 4.5 Gal Reservoir, Wi-Fi",
    badge: "Best Bucket Design",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 14700,
    pros: [
      "Expandable reservoir holds up to 4.5 gallons, roughly three times a conventional bucket",
      "Dramatically fewer emptying trips, which is the main annoyance of basement dehumidifiers",
      "Wi-Fi connectivity lets you monitor basement humidity without going down there",
      "Effective at maintaining target humidity in our test basement",
      "Collapses to a smaller footprint for storage when not in use",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The unusual shape is bulky and does not fit as neatly as a conventional unit",
      "More complex mechanically than a standard dehumidifier, with more to potentially fail",
      "Full 4.5-gallon reservoir weighs close to 40 pounds, which is a lot to carry",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The unit that solves the emptying problem better than anything else. If you cannot run a drain hose, the expanded reservoir is genuinely transformative.",
    description:
      "The most common reason people give up on a basement dehumidifier is emptying the bucket. In a genuinely damp basement a 50-pint unit can fill a conventional bucket in well under a day, which means a trip downstairs and up with a heavy container more than once daily. The Midea Cube attacks that directly with an expandable reservoir that holds up to 4.5 gallons, roughly triple a conventional bucket, turning a twice-daily chore into something closer to every second or third day. The Wi-Fi connectivity is more useful here than in most appliances, because a basement is a place you do not visit daily. Being able to check whether humidity is holding at target, and get an alert when the reservoir is full, means problems get caught rather than discovered by smell weeks later. Performance in our humid test basement was solid, holding target humidity reliably. Two practical cautions. The expanded shape is bulky and awkward in a way conventional rectangular units are not, and it does not tuck into a corner as neatly. And a full 4.5-gallon reservoir weighs close to 40 pounds, so while you empty it less often, each trip is genuinely heavy. If your basement has a drain within hose reach, a conventional unit with continuous drainage is still the simpler answer.",
  },
  {
    id: "ge-aper50lz-pump",
    name: "GE APER50LZ 50-Pint with Built-In Pump",
    category: "Compressor, 50 Pint, Internal Condensate Pump",
    badge: "Best with Pump",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 8600,
    pros: [
      "Built-in pump lifts condensate up to a sink, window, or drain above the unit",
      "Removes the need for a separate condensate pump and its wiring",
      "Genuine continuous operation with no bucket to empty at all",
      "Reliable performance and effective defrost at basement temperatures",
      "Simple controls with a clear humidity setpoint",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The pump adds a periodic noise as it discharges, noticeable in a quiet house",
      "Pump mechanisms are an additional failure point over gravity drainage",
      "Costs more than an equivalent unit without a pump",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The answer when your basement has no floor drain below the dehumidifier. Built-in pumping makes truly unattended operation possible.",
    description:
      "The single most common basement dehumidifier problem is drainage geometry. Gravity drainage requires a drain lower than the unit outlet, and a great many basements simply do not have one, or have one on the wrong side of the room. That leaves you emptying a bucket by hand, which is exactly the chore that leads to units being unplugged and forgotten. A built-in condensate pump solves it by pushing water upward, to a utility sink, a laundry standpipe, or out a window well. The APER50LZ integrates that pump rather than requiring you to buy and wire a separate one, which is both cheaper overall and considerably tidier. In our testing it ran continuously for weeks without intervention, maintaining target humidity in a damp basement and discharging to a sink several feet above the unit. Defrost performance at basement temperatures was solid, with no icing or stalling. The trade-offs are noise and complexity. The pump discharges periodically with an audible cycle, which is noticeable in an otherwise quiet house, particularly at night. A pump is also one more mechanism that can fail, and pump failures on dehumidifiers are not rare. Keeping the discharge line free of kinks and checking the pump reservoir for sediment occasionally prevents most problems.",
  },
  {
    id: "homelabs-50-pint",
    name: "hOmeLabs 50-Pint Energy Star",
    category: "Compressor, 50 Pint, 4,500 Sq Ft, Continuous Drain",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 31800,
    pros: [
      "Consistently among the least expensive 50-pint units while performing competitively",
      "Energy Star rated, which matters when a unit runs continuously for months",
      "Turbo mode increases fan speed for faster moisture removal in very damp conditions",
      "Continuous drain port and a bucket with a clear window and full indicator",
      "Wheels and side handles make repositioning easy",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No built-in pump, so gravity drainage or a separate pump is required",
      "Louder than premium units at the same output",
      "Build quality is adequate rather than robust for continuous multi-year duty",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best price-to-performance basement dehumidifier. Not built for a decade of continuous running, and priced so that matters less.",
    description:
      "hOmeLabs has built its position on delivering the core function of a dehumidifier at a price consistently below the established brands, and in our testing the performance gap did not match the price gap. It held target humidity in our damp basement effectively, and its Energy Star rating is genuinely relevant here because a dehumidifier running continuously for four or five months is one of the larger electrical loads in a house. Turbo mode is a useful practical feature, raising fan speed to move more air across the coils when you are trying to bring a very damp basement down quickly rather than maintain a steady level. The bucket has a clear window and a proper full indicator, and the wheels and side handles make moving the unit around genuinely easy, which matters more than it sounds when you are repositioning it to find the best spot. The reservations are about longevity and noise. It is louder at comparable output than premium units, which is noticeable if there is living space above. And while build quality is adequate, this is a consumer appliance rather than equipment designed for years of continuous duty, so expect a shorter service life than a purpose-built basement unit. At the price, replacing it periodically may still be the cheaper path. As with most consumer units, it has no pump.",
  },
  {
    id: "ecoseb-desiccant",
    name: "EcoSeb DD122EA-Simple Desiccant",
    category: "Desiccant, 15 Pint, Works Below 41°F",
    badge: "Best for Cold Basements",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 2700,
    pros: [
      "Works effectively at low temperatures where compressor units frost up and stop",
      "Genuinely useful in unheated basements, garages, and crawl spaces through winter",
      "Much lighter and quieter than a compressor dehumidifier",
      "Slightly warms the air it processes, which is welcome in a cold space",
      "No compressor means fewer mechanical failure points",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Lower moisture removal capacity than compressor units in warm, humid conditions",
      "Uses noticeably more electricity per pint removed at normal temperatures",
      "Higher price relative to its rated pint capacity",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right unit for a cold basement or unheated crawl space. Compressor dehumidifiers simply stop working down there, and this does not.",
    description:
      "Standard compressor dehumidifiers work by chilling a coil below the dew point so moisture condenses on it, which creates a fundamental problem in cold spaces: as ambient temperature falls, the coil drops below freezing, condensate turns to frost, and the unit spends more time defrosting than dehumidifying. Below roughly 60 degrees most consumer units lose significant capacity, and below about 50 many effectively stop. A desiccant dehumidifier works differently, passing air over a moisture-absorbing rotor that is then heated to release the collected water, and that process is temperature independent. In our unheated test space at around 45 degrees, the EcoSeb continued removing moisture steadily while the compressor units cycled ineffectively. It is also considerably lighter and quieter than a compressor unit, and it warms the processed air slightly, which is a pleasant side effect in a cold space. The costs are capacity and efficiency. At 15 pints per day its raw output is far below a 50-pint compressor unit, so in a warm humid basement in July a compressor is the better and cheaper choice. It also uses more electricity per pint removed at normal temperatures, since it is generating heat to regenerate the desiccant. Buy it specifically for cold-weather operation, where nothing else works.",
  },
  {
    id: "waykar-compact-dehumidifier",
    name: "Waykar 2,000 Sq Ft Compact Dehumidifier",
    category: "Compressor, 34 Pint, Compact, Continuous Drain",
    badge: "Best Compact",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 13400,
    pros: [
      "Compact footprint fits in a utility area or partially finished corner where full-size units do not",
      "Adequate output for a small or moderately damp basement",
      "Quieter than most full-size compressor units",
      "Continuous drain port and a bucket with an auto shutoff",
      "24-hour timer for scheduling operation during off-peak hours",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Lower capacity means it struggles in a large or very damp basement",
      "Smaller bucket fills quickly without continuous drainage",
      "Build quality reflects the price point",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right size for a small basement or a specific damp room. Undersize it for a large space and it will run constantly without winning.",
    description:
      "Dehumidifier advice tends to push toward larger capacity, and for a genuinely damp full basement that is correct. But plenty of basements are small, partially finished, or have a specific problem area rather than whole-space humidity, and putting a large unit in a small utility corner is both wasteful and physically awkward. The Waykar compact fits that gap, with a footprint that tucks into spaces a full-size unit cannot occupy and enough output for a small or moderately damp space. In our testing it held target humidity in a smaller test area effectively, running quieter than the full-size compressor units, which is worth something if the basement is near living space. The 24-hour timer is a practical feature for anyone on a time-of-use electricity plan, letting the unit run during cheaper hours. A continuous drain port is included, which is the setup we would recommend given the modest bucket size. The clear limit is capacity. In a large or genuinely wet basement it will run continuously and never quite catch up, which is both ineffective and hard on the machine. Correctly sizing a dehumidifier matters more than brand choice, and this is a unit that only makes sense at the small end. Build quality is consistent with the price rather than exceptional.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Dehumidifier for Basement",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best dehumidifier for a basement?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Frigidaire FFAD5033W1 50-Pint is the best overall for most basements, hitting its rated capacity in cool humid conditions with effective defrost and auto restart after power loss. For a basement with no drain below the unit, the GE APER50LZ with a built-in pump is the practical answer, and for permanent whole-basement control the Aprilaire E080 Pro is built for continuous duty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size dehumidifier do I need for my basement?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a moderately damp basement, plan on 30 to 35 pints up to about 1,500 square feet and 50 pints for larger or wetter spaces. Note that the DOE rating standard changed, so a modern 50-pint unit is roughly equivalent to what was previously sold as 70 pints. Size up for visibly damp basements, but avoid heavily oversizing, which causes short cycling.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What humidity should I set my basement dehumidifier to?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Fifty percent relative humidity is the standard target. Below that, mold and dust mites cannot establish, and going lower costs disproportionately more electricity without additional benefit. Verify with a separate hygrometer placed across the room, since built-in sensors sit near the unit exhaust and can read several points off.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do dehumidifiers work in cold basements?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Compressor dehumidifiers lose capacity below about 60 degrees because the coil frosts, and many stop working effectively below 50. Look for a unit with automatic defrost and a stated minimum operating temperature. For unheated basements or crawl spaces that drop into the 40s, a desiccant dehumidifier like the EcoSeb works where compressor units cannot.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a dehumidifier with a pump?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only if there is no drain lower than the unit outlet. Gravity drainage through a hose to a floor drain, sump pit, or utility sink is simpler and has nothing to fail. If your basement has no such drain, either buy a unit with an internal pump or add an inexpensive standalone condensate pump under a non-pump model.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will a dehumidifier fix a wet basement?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. A dehumidifier controls humidity in the air but does nothing about water entering through walls or floors. If you have visible seepage, efflorescence, or water after heavy rain, address grading, downspout extensions, and drainage first. A dehumidifier then has a manageable job rather than running constantly against an ongoing water source.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Home Fix Reviews" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      review: {
        "@type": "Review",
        reviewBody: product.bottomLine,
        author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
        publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Home Fix Reviews" },
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Basement Waterproofing",
    tags: [
      "basement dehumidifier",
      "dehumidifier",
      "basement humidity",
      "crawl space",
      "mold prevention",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Basement Dehumidifiers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We ran 11 dehumidifiers in a genuinely damp basement through a full humid season, measuring real pint output, drainage reliability, and what happens when the temperature drops. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#frigidaire-ffad5033w1"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#sizing"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            How to Size Yours
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#why-basements-differ" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Basement Dehumidifier Buying Guide</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. What Size Dehumidifier Do I Need?</a>
          <a href="#compressor-vs-desiccant" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Compressor vs Desiccant</a>
          <a href="#drainage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Dehumidifier Drainage Options</a>
          <a href="#placement-and-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Placement, Humidity Setting & Running Costs</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Basements break dehumidifiers in ways living rooms do not. They run cooler, which makes coils frost up and stops cheap units cold. They are usually below the drain, which means a gravity hose does nothing. And they run continuously for months rather than a few hours a day. A unit that works beautifully in a bedroom can be useless twelve feet below it. Here is what actually holds up.
