        <!-- SECTION 3: What Size Dehumidifier Do I Need for My Basement? -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Dehumidifier Do I Need for My Basement?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dehumidifier capacity is quoted in pints of water removed per day, and the figure is more confusing than it looks because the test conditions changed a few years ago.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rating Standard Changed</h3>

          <p>
            Department of Energy test conditions were revised, and the newer standard tests at a lower temperature that produces lower numbers for the same machine. A unit rated 70 pints under the old standard is roughly a 50-pint unit under the new one. Practically, this means a modern 50-pint dehumidifier is equivalent to what used to be sold as a 70-pint model, and old sizing advice quoting 70-pint units for basements now points at 50-pint models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sizing by Area and Dampness</h3>

          <p>
            For a moderately damp basement, plan on roughly 30 to 35 pints for spaces up to about 1,500 square feet, and 50 pints for larger or wetter spaces. For a basement that is visibly wet, has standing moisture, or smells strongly musty, size up rather than down. An undersized unit runs continuously without reaching target humidity, which wastes electricity and wears the machine out faster than a correctly sized one working intermittently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Deliberately Oversize Either</h3>

          <p>
            A heavily oversized unit short-cycles, switching on and off frequently, which is hard on the compressor and produces less consistent humidity than a properly matched unit running longer cycles. Match capacity to the space rather than buying the largest available.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Set Humidity to 50 Percent</h3>

          <p>
            Below about 50 percent relative humidity, mold and dust mites cannot establish. Below about 40 percent, the unit works considerably harder for benefits you will not notice, and wood furniture and trim can dry excessively. Fifty percent is the standard target and the setting we would use in almost every basement.
          </p>

