              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a dehumidifier with a pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if there is no drain lower than the unit outlet. Gravity drainage through a hose to a floor drain, sump pit, or utility sink is simpler and has nothing to fail. If your basement has no such drain, either buy a unit with an internal pump or add an inexpensive standalone condensate pump under a non-pump model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier fix a wet basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A dehumidifier controls humidity in the air but does nothing about water entering through walls or floors. If you have visible seepage, efflorescence, or water after heavy rain, address grading, downspout extensions, and drainage first. A dehumidifier then has a manageable job rather than running constantly against an ongoing water source.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
