        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Basement Dehumidifier Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Basement dehumidifier failures follow a very consistent pattern, and most of them are about setup rather than the machine itself.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the Bucket</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 50-pint unit in a damp basement removes over six gallons a day, which means emptying a bucket more than once daily. Nobody sustains that, and the near-universal result is a unit that gets unplugged and forgotten by August. Set up gravity drainage or a pump from day one, before the novelty wears off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Unit That Cannot Handle Basement Temperatures</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many consumer dehumidifiers are rated only down to about 65 degrees, which is warmer than a lot of basements ever get. Below their rated range the coil frosts and the unit cycles ineffectively while appearing to run. Check the stated minimum operating temperature and confirm the unit has automatic defrost before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Humidity When the Problem Is Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier running continuously against active seepage will never reach its setpoint, wears itself out, and costs a fortune to run. Visible dampness on walls, white efflorescence, or water after rain means the fix is grading, downspouts, and drainage. Solve the water first and the dehumidifier becomes an easy job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pushing It Into a Corner Behind Storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dehumidifiers need clear airflow into the intake and out of the exhaust, and most specify six to twelve inches of clearance. Wedged into a corner behind boxes, a perfectly good unit will underperform badly and people conclude it is too small. Place it centrally with space around it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

